WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.670
- In this video, we're
gonna learn how to use

00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:09.477
the software called ImageJ.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:12.650
And the first thing you need
to do is go to this website

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:14.993
written here to access
the software online.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.530
We're gonna use a file,
picture of a hair follicle

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:25.530
to demonstrate how to
open an image in ImageJ

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.147
and stack some different layers of images

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:38.260
into one image, add color,

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:42.003
and adjust the brightness
and contrast in these images.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:44.760
And so what you can do
is just take the file

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:47.290
and drag it into the window.

00:00:47.290 --> 00:00:49.440
And when you drop it,
the file should open.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.093
Oh, there it is.

00:00:58.810 --> 00:01:03.530
So here you see a stacked
image for four channels

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.480
that we took with the
florescence microscope,

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:09.173
and each channel was taken
with a different marker.

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:12.760
And you can see here
that there's a scroll bar

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.860
at the bottom showing,

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:17.470
now it's showing just one of the channels.

00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:20.270
And if you scroll, you can see
all the different channels.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.653
So in this picture, you
have a total of four.

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:27.174
And so now each channel
shows you individually

00:01:27.174 --> 00:01:31.090
what the images look like.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.630
But what we wanna do is see
all of the channels together

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.040
and assign them different colors.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:39.210
And the one quick way
to do this is to go here

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:44.210
in the image tab under color,
and click make composite.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:46.840
So once you do that,

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:49.870
you can see here now that all
four channels are visible,

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.920
each of them assigned
with a different color.

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:55.573
So you see red, green, blue, and white.

00:01:56.689 --> 00:01:59.820
So for us to see all of these channels,

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.710
if you can, so we can
individually turn them

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:07.070
on and off, can go back to image, color,

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:08.523
and click on channels tool.

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:13.000
Once you do with that
this window will appear.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:16.130
And it shows you like
the four channels here.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:21.130
So the first channel is,
if we turn it on and off,

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.070
you can see that it's the red channel.

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.650
And channel two is the green one,

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:28.487
channel three is the blue one,

00:02:28.487 --> 00:02:31.533
and channel four is the white one.

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:34.811
And so you can play with these channels,

00:02:34.811 --> 00:02:35.644
turn them on and off.

00:02:35.644 --> 00:02:37.323
For example, you could
only see channel one,

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.740
then you can see only the
red, and see channel one

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.790
and two only, you only see red and green.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.230
And so we can turn them
on and off like that.

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:51.080
So now what we want to do
is to adjust the brightness

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.390
and contrast of different channels.

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:57.100
And so now you go here to image, adjust,

00:02:57.100 --> 00:02:59.303
and here there's some brightness contrast.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.040
So with scientific images,
normally we actually

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:06.400
don't adjust brightness
and contrast per se,

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.123
but rather the minimum
and the maximum signal

00:03:09.123 --> 00:03:12.750
that we allow to be visible in the image.

00:03:12.750 --> 00:03:15.570
And so you can see here
that there's a histogram

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.860
of a color red, indicating that
you are in the red channel.

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.410
So for example, when we
move across channels,

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:24.210
we can for example, the second channel,

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:25.600
we know it's the green one,

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.723
you can see that this changes as well.

00:03:28.723 --> 00:03:29.986
And you move again.

00:03:29.986 --> 00:03:32.480
Now we're in the blue
channel, this changes as well.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:35.270
So let's say we want to make
the red channel brighter.

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.640
So first we go to the correct channel,

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.500
and you will see here again the color.

00:03:39.500 --> 00:03:44.010
We're in the red channel and to adjust,

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:45.970
to make it brighter, what you can do

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:48.590
is decrease the maximum here.

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:51.480
And so, as you move along,

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.720
you can see their red getting
brighter and brighter,

00:03:53.720 --> 00:03:57.010
and like right here, like,
we're at a very saturated point,

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:00.300
which usually you don't want
with scientific imaging.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.693
So maybe we'll just move it down to here.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.210
And so, if you want to see it clearer

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:09.588
you can just turn off the other channels,

00:04:09.588 --> 00:04:12.150
and then you can do the same thing.

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.700
You can adjust it here.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.790
So now let's try to do it

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:19.663
in a different channel, say the green one.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.660
And so I have to move
again, the channel here,

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.840
and now you're at the green one.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.330
If you want to make it brighter again,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.860
just move it, the maximum down.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.480
Now you start to see that actually

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:33.550
there's background signal here.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:35.450
Where there's actually no real signal,

00:04:35.450 --> 00:04:38.830
but there's some auto fluorescence.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.170
And so sometimes what you
want to do is to remove

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.040
that background signal to
make the image look nicer.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.000
And for that to happen, now we adjust,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:48.970
instead, the minimum, you
can increase the minimum,

00:04:48.970 --> 00:04:51.430
such that you don't see the
background signal anymore.

00:04:51.430 --> 00:04:52.733
Like that, for example.

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:56.740
And then we can put
back the other channels,

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:00.950
and now you see how the
different channels interact.

00:05:00.950 --> 00:05:03.690
So right now you see that
the blue is very dim,

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.740
so we can adjust that too.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.270
Go to the blue channel,

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:10.410
and just decrease the maximum
to increase the signal.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.303
And now you can actually see
the blue dots up here in.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.420
And so let's say you don't like
the colors of these images.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.780
So say I wanna, for example,

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:25.180
the last channel, which is white here,

00:05:25.180 --> 00:05:27.300
it's actually a nuclear
stain, which typically

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:32.300
in most scientific
displays is shown as blue.

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.220
And so let's say we want
to change this into blue,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.400
but actually we know that
channel three is already blue.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.320
So say we want to switch their colors.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:42.230
Let's making the channel
three white instead.

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:44.484
So now we're in channel
three, you can see here

00:05:44.484 --> 00:05:47.151
because it's the blue
channel and yes, the third.

00:05:47.151 --> 00:05:48.583
In here.

00:05:49.510 --> 00:05:51.510
Just go here, to more,

00:05:51.510 --> 00:05:52.960
and here you can see the different colors

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.780
that you can put them in.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:56.220
And now we're going to
change them to white,

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:57.760
which is actually called grays here.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:00.330
So just click that, and
you see now suddenly

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.420
the blues became white.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.720
And then now, but we now
we have two white channels

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.390
channel three and channel four.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:08.870
So now I want to switch
channel four into blue.

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:10.930
So you can just go with the
channel four, the last one here

00:06:10.930 --> 00:06:15.103
in the scroll bar, and then
do the same thing, click blue.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:17.730
And now that became blue.

00:06:17.730 --> 00:06:19.250
But now that's quite dim actually.

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:23.440
So now we can adjust that
again to increase the CML

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:26.330
so we can see them naturally.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:29.370
Now the white, because white
looks brighter to the eye.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:31.040
I think it's a bit too bright now.

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:34.993
So let's just decrease
that to make it look nicer.

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.100
So let's say we're happy with this image.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:43.070
So what you want to do,
if you save it just as is,

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.670
it will actually not change anything.

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:47.800
It will still save as the same exact file

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:48.900
that you started with.

00:06:50.060 --> 00:06:52.960
So what you want to do
to save the colored image

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:57.610
is just go to image,
color, and stack to RGB.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:00.903
This will compile all the colors
together in one flat image.

00:07:01.990 --> 00:07:03.360
And so now you have this new file,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.970
which is the hair follicle RGB,

00:07:07.970 --> 00:07:10.970
but actually what you see here,

00:07:10.970 --> 00:07:12.540
let's just move that out of the way,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.940
that you don't have the scroll bar here,

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.260
because all the channels
are merged together.

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:19.840
And so, for example, if you just go here,

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:23.217
you see that the brightness
contrast only has one thing.

00:07:23.217 --> 00:07:24.630
And if you move that around

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.623
it just adjust all the colors together.

00:07:28.310 --> 00:07:32.100
So, if you're happy with this
image now you can save it.

00:07:32.100 --> 00:07:37.100
Go to file, save as TIF,
and you can change the name.

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:38.973
Let's see.

00:07:38.973 --> 00:07:40.130
No access to the TIF.

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:41.430
So let's just remove that.

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.340
Press okay.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.050
And that should save in your computer.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:48.100
So I'm just going to open it.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:51.320
So if we go back here
to the original image,

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.570
if you click on that and
just try to preview it

00:07:53.570 --> 00:07:54.553
in your computer.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.800
Well, it's loading.

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.360
You see that actually,
you don't see anything

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.860
because this image has not been processed.

00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:12.640
You cannot really see it with
regular viewing applications,

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:14.190
like the one that you see here.

00:08:15.370 --> 00:08:17.000
But now if we go to the new one,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:18.250
which we just downloaded.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:24.240
Which is here, double click
that, just to view it,

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:26.210
now you can actually see it.

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.440
And then this, you can
use in any application

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.823
like PowerPoint, or whichever you want.

00:08:34.065 --> 00:08:38.537
So yeah, now you know
how to adjust the colors

00:08:38.537 --> 00:08:40.794
and the brightness of images,

00:08:40.794 --> 00:08:44.370
especially with scientific
images that are usually a stack

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:48.883
of multiple colors in this
software called ImageJ.

